## Script to create output plots

Below is a template of a script how to creates data points. You will have to 
amend the prefix accordingly.
Note that we really only are interested in one line from the output and that's 
the time to solution that is spent on the actual solver, i.e. bare any I/O and 
mesh generation.


@todo Script needs work

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PREFIX=tbb or omp or sycl

export CELL_SIZE=1.8

OUTPUT=results/$PREFIX.txt
echo $PREFIX > $OUTPUT
for AMR in without-amr with-amr
do
  for TREES in 1 6 8 12
  do
    for SCHEDULER in native-no-priorities native tailored parallel-for subtasks subtasks-and-kernel-parallelisation
    do
      FILE=results/$PREFIX-$AMR-$SCHEDULER-$TREES-trees-$CELL_SIZE.txt
      echo $FILE >> $OUTPUT
      grep "time stepping:" $FILE >> $OUTPUT
    done
  done
done
echo wrote all data to $OUTPUT

tar -cf $PREFIX-$SCHEDULER-without-amr-$TREES-trees.tar --files-from /dev/null
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The last line packs the data from one upscaling exercise into a tar archive.
Single files are obviously more convenient to move around between computers, 
but archives are notably directly supported by Peano's postprocessing scripts.


## Plotting performance data

Get the tar archives. After that, we can use the out-of-the-box Peano scripts
to generate the plots

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PREFIX=omp
export TREES=8

OUTPUT=$PREFIX-$TREES-trees-without-amr
python3 ../../../../python/exahype2/postprocessing/plot-scaling.py --max-cores-per-rank=-1 --log-x --log-y --output $OUTPUT \
  --label "native tasking, subtasks, subtasks and parallel kernels" --title "No AMR" \
  $PREFIX-native-without-amr-$TREES-trees.tar.gz \
  $PREFIX-subtasks-without-amr-$TREES-trees.tar.gz \
  $PREFIX-subtasks-and-kernel-parallelisation-without-amr-$TREES-trees.tar.gz

export OUTPUT=$PREFIX-$TREES-trees-with-amr
python3 ../../../../python/exahype2/postprocessing/plot-scaling.py --max-cores-per-rank=-1 --log-x --log-y --output $OUTPUT \
  --label "native tasking, subtasks, subtasks and parallel kernels" --title "AMR, patch size 3, cell size 1.8" \
  $PREFIX-native-without-amr-$TREES-trees.tar.gz \
  $PREFIX-subtasks-without-amr-$TREES-trees.tar.gz \
  $PREFIX-subtasks-and-kernel-parallelisation-without-amr-$TREES-trees.tar.gz
 
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

which clearly showcase that the kernel parallelisation pays off even though 
the overall scalability is poor with the present rather coarse mesh:



@image html benchmarks/exahype2/ccz4/single-black-hole/omp-8-trees-with-amr-cs-1.8-ps-3.png
@image html benchmarks/exahype2/ccz4/single-black-hole/omp-8-trees-with-amr-cs-0.6-ps-6.png



# Some other setups


Here are some canonical settings:


Description     | Solver         | Cell size | End time | Patch size | Remarks   | Build command
----------------|----------------|-----------|----------|------------|-----------|---------------
Minimal setup   | Finite Volumes | 5.4       | 1.0      |          6 | Toy problem with 729 cells. Tells you if the code, in principle, works. Should crash at one point suddenly as the eigenvalue becomes zero, which showcases that this setup is too coarse to capture anything that has a physical meaning. | ```python3 performance-studies.py -s fv-6 -et 1.0 --plot 0.0 --cell-size 5.4```
Simplest FV setup for single node workstation (laptop)  | Finite Volumes | 1.8       | 1e-1     |          6 | You cannot see AMR here, but already gives you 19,683 cells. Load balancing should be really good. This is kind of the largest regular mesh setup that I can fit on a local laptop. | ```python3 performance-studies.py -s fv-6 -et 0.1 --plot 0.0 --cell-size 1.8```




